From: PS5_Proton
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: merge a program's sequential file writes for up to 15 ms on the PS5

A Unity game's download in a launcher ran at 2.5 MB/s on a 1 Gbps line. The
network gives 19 to 35 MB/s on this console; the disk (bfs under a nullfs)
takes 2.3 ms per write() plus 0.3 ms per 64 KiB, and the Agent wrote its 1 GiB
in 17,588 calls of 64 KiB or less, 5 to 10 MiB/s. Writing through a mapping
was tried and rejected (it skips block allocation: other readers get stale
bytes). A 256 KiB write costs 3.5 ms (74 MB/s), so fewer calls is the lever.

NtWriteFile on a regular file keeps sequential writes of up to 256 KiB in a
per-process buffer (4 files, 512 KiB each) and writes them as one at 256 KiB,
after 15 ms (a thread with every signal blocked), or when the process does
anything that could show the file: a read, query, set-information, flush or
control on a handle on it, any open, delete or attribute or directory query, a
section made of it (and no merging for that file after), a handle on it closed
or duplicated, a process created or ended. Not merged: write-through handles,
appends, writes over 256 KiB, writes not next to the buffered bytes (the
buffer goes first, so the order holds). The only difference from Windows that
remains: another process that opens or reads the file within 15 ms of a write
sees the bytes late, a process killed from outside loses at most those 15 ms,
and a write that fails at flush time (disk full) is logged, not reported, as
with Windows' lazy writer. The helper census counts merged calls and bytes,
flushes and the time in them.
---
